h LLM-as-a-judge human expert and GPT-4 pairwise judgments\",\n490\t          \"rationale\": \"Provides the expert-human and LLM-judge evaluation designs with only 6 models, contrasting with crowd and sports data.\"\n491\t        },\n492\t        {\n493\t          \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n494\t          \"query\": \"Elo rating versus Bradley-Terry sports ranking NBA game outcomes\",\n495\t          \"rationale\": \"Sports family (NBA, 100+ teams) is the fourth design; need rating-system background distinguishing Elo from batch BT.\"\n496\t        },\n497\t        {\n498\t          \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n499\t          \"query\": \"sensitivity of leaderboard rankings to evaluation data choices statistical uncertainty of model rankings\",\n500\t          \"rationale\": \"Cross-design comparison and noise calibration of rank stability requires literature on ranking uncertainty and leaderboard reliability.\"\n501\t        }\n502\t      ],\n503\t      \"result_driven_themes\": [\n504\t        \"Run produced no scored winner over the baseline (investigation completed_no_winner; baseline combined score 0.914 retained), so related work should support framing around evaluator-verified minimal-perturbation sensitivity and its limits, not claims of a superior search method\",\n505\t        \"Sensitivity differs across evaluation designs (crowd, expert, LLM-judge, sports) and must be calibrated against noise and permuted-input controls\",\n506\t        \"Rank-1 identity is fragile when the top-two gap is small relative to ridge-regularised BT uncertainty\"\n507\t      ],\n508\t      \"status\": \"ready\"\n509\t    },\n510\t    {\n511\t      \"architectural_shifts\": [\n512\t        \"Shift from one-shot first-order influence ranking to iterative re-linearisation with exact-refit verification of candidate edit sets\"\n513\t      ],\n514\t      \"benchmarks\": [\n515\t        \"Chatbot Arena human preference (arena-human-preference-55k)\",\n516\t        \"MT-Bench human and GPT-4 judgments\",\n517\t        \"NBA historical Elo game records (FiveThirtyEight)\"\n518\t      ],\n519\t      \"central_topic\": \"Sensitivity of Bradley-Terry pairwise-comparison leaderboard rankings (rank-1 displacement) to small, evaluator-verified drop/flip perturbations of the comparison data, compared across evaluation designs (crowd votes, expert human judgments, LLM judges, sports).\",\n520\t      \"comparators\": [\n521\t        \"First-order influence baseline (Koh & Liang style) as the starter candidate\",\n522\t        \"Random, win-rate-heuristic and constant edit baselines\",\n523\t        \"Vote-rigging / manipulation strategies on crowdsourced Arena leaderboards\",\n524\t        \"Elo / online rating systems versus batch Bradley-Terry\"\n525\t      ],\n526\t      \"core_methods\": [\n527\t        \"Ridge-penalised Bradley-Terry maximum-likelihood fitting of pairwise comparisons\",\n528\t        \"Influence-function / first-order approximation to find the data points whose removal changes a fitted parameter\",\n529\t        \"Approximate maximum-influence perturbation (drop-k) robustness metrics for statistical conclusions\",\n530\t        \"Iterative re-linearised greedy drop/flip edit search with exact-refit selection and prefix line search (attempted candidate variants; no scored result yet)\",\n531\t        \"Adversarial data poisoning via label flips against\n532\t\n533\t[truncated after 14000 chars]\n534\t```\n535\t\n536\t## manuscript source excerpt\n537\t\n538\t```tex\n539\t\\documentclass{article}\n540\t\n541\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n542\t\\usepackage{xfp}\n543\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n544\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n545\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{\n546\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n547\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}\n548\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}\n549\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n550\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}\n551\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}\n552\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}\n553\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}\n554\t}\n555\t\n556\t \n557\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n558\t\n559\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n560\t\\usepackage{url}\n561\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n562\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n563\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n564\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n565\t\n566\t\\newcommand{\\ts}{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\n567\t\\newcommand{\\sig}{\\sigma}\n568\t\n569\t\\title{How Few Edits Displace the Top of a Bradley--Terry Leaderboard? A Verified First-Order Influence Probe on Crowd, Expert, LLM-Judge and Sports Comparison Data}\n570\t\\author{Anonymous authors}\n571\t\n572\t\\begin{document}\n573\t\\maketitle\n574\t\n575\t\\begin{abstract}\n576\tPairwise-comparison leaderboards rank competitors by fitting a Bradley--Terry model to many ``A versus B'' judgments, yet it is unclear how few comparisons must change before the top-ranked competitor loses its place, and whether this differs between evaluation designs.\n577\tWe measure this for a ridge-penalised Bradley--Terry fit, a single target (the rank-1 competitor), two edit modes (dropping rows or reversing outcomes) and budgets from 0.1\\% to 2\\% of rows.\n578\tEdits are proposed by a one-shot first-order influence search and scored by a trusted evaluator that refits the model on the edited data.\n579\tOn held-out data from four designs (crowd votes, expert human judgments, LLM-judge judgments, basketball results) the family-averaged edit success score is 0.606, with per-design scores of 1.000, 0.833, 0.374 and 0.216, respectively.\n580\tAt the 2\\% budget the search displaces rank 1 in every crowd and sports case, in no LLM-judge case, and for expert human data only by reversing outcomes.\n581\tA simple win-rate heuristic scores 0.574.\n582\tThese are bounded measurements of one heuristic attack on one target; they carry no sampling uncertainty, and the cause of the design ordering is untested.\n583\t\\end{abstract}\n584\t\n585\t\\section{Introduction}\n586\tPublic model-evaluation leaderboards often aggregate a large number of pairwise judgments (which of two responses, models or teams was better) into a Bradley--Terry (BT) score for every competitor, and then publish a ranking \\citep{Bradley1952Rank,Chiang2024Chatbot}.\n587\tThese rankings guide model selection, release announcements and research comparisons, so their dependence on the underlying comparison data matters.\n588\tTwo lines of concern motivate this paper.\n589\tFirst, data are noisy and finite, so a small difference between the top two competitors may not be reliable \\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}.\n590\tSecond, crowd-sourced leaderboards can be influenced deliberately, for instance through vote rigging \\citep{Min2025Rigging} or through differences in data access and sampling between providers \\citep{Singh2025Illusion}.\n591\tBoth concerns lead to the same operational question: \\emph{how many comparison rows have to be edited before the top-ranked competitor is no longer ranked first?}\n592\t\n593\tWe study this question for a deliberately narrow, fully specified setting.\n594\tThe leaderboard is a ridge-penalised BT fit; the target is the competitor ranked first on the unedited data; an edit either drops a row or reverses its outcome; and the edit budget is between 0.1\\% and 2\\% of the rows.\n595\tA trusted evaluator, which never uses any quantity reported by the proposer, refits the model on the edited data and records whether rank 1 was displaced or, if not, what fraction of the top-versus-runner-up gap was closed.\n596\tThe edits themselves are proposed by a one-shot first-order influence search in the spirit of influence functions \\citep{Koh2017Understanding} and approximate-robustness analysis of ``dropping a little data'' \\citep{Broderick2020Automatic}.\n597\tWe apply it to held-out data from four evaluation designs that differ in who judges and what is judged: crowd-sourced human votes on chatbot responses \\citep{Chiang2024Chatbot}, expert human judgments and GPT-4 judgments on the same MT-Bench comparisons \\citep{Zheng2023Judging}, and National Basketball Association (NBA) game results.\n598\tFigure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarises the protocol.\n599\t\n600\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n601\t\\centering\n602\t\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/overview_protocol.pdf}\n603\t\\caption{Evaluation protocol. Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fitted with a ridge Bradley--Terry model, which fixes the rank-1 competitor and its gap $G$ to the runner-up. A proposer returns at most $k$ rows to drop or reverse, with $k$ from 0.1\\% to 2\\% of the rows. The evaluator refits the edited data, reports whether rank 1 changed, and otherwise reports the fraction of the gap closed.}\n604\t\\label{fig:overview}\n605\t\\end{figure}\n606\t\n607\t\\paragraph{What this paper is and is not.}\n608\tThe studied search is the standard first-order influence heuristic, not a new algorithm, and we do not claim an improvement over any baseline: the evaluator's own influence baseline uses the same first-order formulas and obtains the same family-averaged score ($0.606$) as the studied search, so the margin over the best reference policy is exactly zero (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n609\tThree alternative search variants were designed but produced no scored evidence and therefore support no comparison (Appendix~\\ref{app:selection}).\n610\tThe contribution is a controlled, verified measurement of how a fixed adversarial probe fares on four evaluation designs, together with a careful statement of what that measurement does and does not show.\n611\tConcretely:\n612\t\\begin{itemize}\n613\t\\item We specify an operational, evaluator-verified protocol (exact refit, five budgets, two edit modes, one target) that makes rank-1 vulnerability comparable across designs, and we derive the first-order effect of dropping or reversing a row, including an identity relating the two modes (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n614\t\\item We report that the probe's success is strongly design dependent: edit success scores of 1.000 (crowd), 0.833 (sports), 0.374 (expert human) and 0.216 (LLM judge), and 2\\%-budget displacement in all crowd and sports cases but in no LLM-judge case (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n615\t\\item We document the validity checks that were run and the uncertainty that is unavailable, and we separate what the numbers show from hypotheses about the cause of the ordering, none of which was tested (Sections~\\ref{sec:analysis} and~\\ref{sec:limits}).\n616\t\\end{itemize}\n617\t\n618\t\\section{Related Work}\n619\t\\paragraph{Bradley--Terry models and their estimation.}\n620\tThe BT model \\citep{Bradley1952Rank} assigns each competitor a latent score and models the win probability of a pair as a logistic function of the score difference. Extensions handle ties \\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, order effects \\citep{Davidson1977OnET}, covariates \\citep{Yan2025InferenceIA}, temporal drift \\citep{Tian2023ASA}, continuous outcomes \\citep{Stern2011ModeratedPC,Fageot2023GeneralizedBM} and Bayesian priors \\citep{Leonard1977AnAB}. Sports results are commonly rated with Elo-style systems that are closely related to BT-type likelihoods; recent work generalises Elo to the margin of victory \\citep{Szczecinski2020GElo} and proposes a score-driven rating system for sports \\citep{Holy2026ScoreDriven}. We fit a static BT model to NBA game outcomes only as a sports instance of pairwise-comparison data, and do not evaluate any Elo-style updating rule. The model has also been used to derive priorities from comparison matrices \\citep{Genest1999DerivingPF} and has been related to PageRank \\citep{Selby2024PageRankAT}. Maximum likelihood estimation requires conditions on the comparison graph \\citep{Butler2004TheEO,Bong2021GeneralizedRF}, which motivates penalised fits \\citep{Varin2024TractableRR}; our evaluator uses a ridge penalty and counts a tie as half a win for each side, a convention in the spirit of tie-extended models. Algorithms for fitting generalised BT models have been analysed by \\citet{Vojnovic2019ConvergenceRO}, and the large-sample behaviour of the estimator when the number of competitors grows by \\citet{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT} and \\citet{Li2021ThePO}.\n621\t\n622\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty of rankings.}\n623\tStatistical work quantifies how sampling noise affects BT scores and ranks, through confidence regions for the score vector \\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}, hypothesis tests \\citep{Makur2024MinimaxHT} and estimators of partial rankings from sparse, noisy comparisons \\citep{Morel-Balbi2025EstimationOP}. These analyses describe average-case sampling variation under a generative model. We instead ask a worst-case, data-conditional question about a single observed dataset: whether a small number of specific rows can change the top rank. The two views are complementary, and our measurements do not substitute for either confidence intervals or noise calibration.\n624\t\n625\t\\paragraph{Influence and robustness to dropping data.}\n626\tInfluence functions estimate the effect of removing or perturbing a training point without refitting \\citep{Koh2017Understanding}. \\citet{Broderick2020Automatic} propose a finite-sample robustness metric asking whether dropping a small fraction of the data can change a conclusion, and use first-order influence approximations to find the data to drop. Our search is of this family, specialised to the top-versus-runner-up score gap of a BT fit and extended to outcome reversal. The linear approximation behind such analyses is the infinitesimal jackknife, which \\citet{Giordano2018Swiss} develop as a general approximation to refits with data removed; its accuracy is a property of the problem and of the size of the perturbation, and is not guaranteed for the 2\\% edits studied here. As in that literature, a first-order search finds candidate edit sets only; we therefore let the evaluator verify every edit by an exact refit.\n627\t\n628\t\\paragraph{Data poisoning and label-flip attacks.}\n629\tChoosing a small set of training points to manipulate so as to change a fitted model is the subject of data-poisoning research; influence-based reasoning has also been used on the defensive side, against poisoning in online learning \\citep{Seetharaman2021Influence}. For preference data, \\citet{Yang2026Preference} present a preference-poisoning attack on offline reinforcement learning from human feedback. We know these two works only from their titles and describe them no further. Our reversal mode is a label-flip edit and our drop mode a deletion, and the first-order scores in Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} are likewise additive per row. The setting differs: the model is a Bradley--Terry leaderboard rather than a preference-optimised language model, the objective is to dislodge one rank rather than to steer parameters towards a target, and an evaluator refits the exact model for verification. We cite this literature for positioning only and do not compare against any poisoning method, and we have not surveyed attacks on rank aggregation exhaustively.\n630\t\n631\t\\paragraph{Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards.}\n632\t\\citet{Chiang2024Chatbot} describe the Chatbot Arena platform, in which users vote between two anonymous model responses and the votes are aggregated into rankings. \\citet{Min2025Rigging} study strategies by which votes on such platforms can be manipulated to change rankings, and \\citet{Singh2025Illusion} analyse evaluation practices on the same platform, including private testing and differences in data access and sampling across providers. \\citet{Zheng2023Judging} release expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments on MT-Bench. We use the Arena and MT-Bench comparison data, but our attack is a generic, evaluator-verified probe; we do not model any platform's actual voting interface or an adversary's access to it.\n633\t\n634\t\\section{Problem Setting and Method}\n635\t\\label{sec:method}\n636\t\\subsection{Leaderboard, target and edit modes}\n637\tA comparison dataset is a list of $n$ rows $(a_i,b_i,y_i)$ over $K$ competitors, with $y_i\\in\\{+1,-1,0\\}$ meaning that $a_i$ wins, $b_i$ wins, or the pair tied. Write $\\tilde y_i\\in\\{1,\\tfrac12,0\\}$ for the corresponding win score of $a_i$. The evaluator fits $\\ts\\in\\mathbb{R}^K$ by maximising the penalised log-likelihood\n638\t\\begin{equation}\n639\t\\ell(\\ts)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\Big[\\tilde y_i\\log\\sig(\\theta_{a_i}-\\theta_{b_i})+(1-\\tilde y_i)\\log\\sig(\\theta_{b_i}-\\theta_{a_i})\\Big]-\\tfrac{\\lambda}{2}\\sum_{k}\\theta_k^2,\\qquad \\lambda=10^{-3},\n640\t\\label{eq:bt}\n641\t\\end{equation}\n642\tto gradient tolerance $10^{-10}$. A tie therefore counts as half a win for each side. The rank-1 competitor is $\\mathrm{top}=\\arg\\max_k\\hat\\theta_k$, the runner-up is $\\mathrm{rv}=\\arg\\max_{k\\neq \\mathrm{top}}\\hat\\theta_k$, and the \\emph{gap} is $G(\\ts)=\\theta_{\\mathrm{top}}-\\max_{j\\neq \\mathrm{top}}\\theta_j$. \n643\tThe target is to make the unedited rank-1 competitor no longer rank first after the edit, that is, to drive $G$ below zero on the refit of the edited data.\n644\tThere are two edit modes. In \\emph{drop} mode, the chosen rows are removed. In \\emph{flip} mode, the outcomes of the chosen rows are reversed, which is only allowed for decisive rows ($y_i\\neq 0$). We call the second mode ``reversal'' in prose and use the evaluator's name ``flip'' only for the mode label, to avoid confusion with displacement rates.\n645\tThe budget is $k=\\max(1,\\lceil fn\\rceil)$ with $f\\in\\{0.1\\%,0.25\\%,0.5\\%,1\\%,2\\%\\}$ of the rows.\\footnote{The five budget fractions are fixed in the evaluator's budget ladder.} \n646\tThe proposer receives the raw arrays with anonymised competitor indices and returns at most $k$ distinct row indices; any malformed answer scores zero for that budget.\n647\t\n648\t\\subsection{First-order influence edit search}\n649\t\\label{sec:influence}\n650\tThe studied search fits $\\hat\\ts$ once, and then ranks rows by the first-order change of the gap that editing each row would cause. Let $p_i=\\sig(\\hat\\theta_{a_i}-\\hat\\theta_{b_i})$ and let $A=\\sum_i p_i(1-p_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})^{\\top}+\\lambda I$ be the negative Hessian of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt}. The score gradient contributed by row $i$ is $(\\tilde y_i-p_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})$. Removing the row perturbs the stationarity condition by $-(\\tilde y_i-p_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})$, so a Newton step gives the parameter change $A^{-1}$ times that perturbation. Define the sensitivity direction and the row coefficient\n651\t\\begin{equation}\n652\tv=A^{-1}(e_{\\mathrm{top}}-e_{\\mathrm{rv}}),\\qquad s_i=v_{a_i}-v_{b_i}.\n653\t\\end{equation}\n654\tThe first-order change of the (top versus runner-up) gap is then\n655\t\\begin{equation}\n656\t\\Delta_i^{\\mathrm{drop}}=-s_i\\,(\\tilde y_i-p_i),\\qquad\n657\t\\Delta_i^{\\mathrm{flip}}=s_i\\,(1-2\\tilde y_i),\n658\t\\label{eq:effects}\n659\t\\end{equation}\n660\twhere the second expression is the effect of replacing $\\tilde y_i$ by $1-\\tilde y_i$. The search returns the (at most) $k$ rows with the most negative $\\Delta_i$ among admissible rows (all rows for drop, decisive rows for flip), discarding rows with $\\Delta_i\\ge 0$ and breaking ties by row index, so the output is deterministic. The implementation fits $\\hat\\ts$ by Newton iterations on the $K\\times K$ pair-count matrix, then solves a single $K\\times K$ linear system for $v$. \n661\t\n662\tThree properties of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} shape how the results should be read.\n663\t\\emph{(i) Additivity.} The per-row effects are summed without modelling how $p_i$ and $A$ change after many rows are edited, so the heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2\\% budget even when it is accurate for one row; the accuracy of the underlying linear approximation is problem dependent \\citep{Giordano2018Swiss}, and we did not evaluate it for these data.\n664\t\\emph{(ii) A fixed rival.} The direction is built for the initial runner-up only. If an edit set raises a different competitor above the incumbent, the search does not anticipate it, although the evaluator would credit the displacement.\n665\t\\emph{(iii) Flip versus drop.} Reversing a row is equivalent to dropping it and inserting the opposite outcome, so $\\Delta^{\\mathrm{flip}}_i=\\Delta^{\\mathrm{drop}}_i+s_i\\,((1-\\tilde y_i)-p_i)$. For a decisive row, $\\tilde y_i-p_i$ has the sign of $\\tilde y_i-\\tfrac12$, so the two modes select gap-reducing rows by the same sign condition on $s_i$, and the flip effect is larger by the factor $1/|\\tilde y_i-p_i|>1$. At first order, reversal is therefore the stronger edit for the same number of rows; this is a statement about Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects}, not an empirical finding.\n666\t\n667\t\\subsection{Scoring and verification}\n668\tFor each budget level, the evaluator refits the edited data with its own damped-Newton fitter and ignores any quantity the proposer reports about gaps or ranks. The score of a level is $1$ if the original top competitor is no longer first, and otherwise $\\mathrm{clip}(1-G_{\\text{after}}/G_{\\text{before}},0,1)$, the fraction of the gap that was closed. The \\emph{edit success score} of a case is the mean over the five budgets and over evaluation seeds; the score of a design is the mean over its cases; and the \\emph{family-averaged} edit success is the equally weighted ($0.25$ each) mean over the four designs. The \\emph{top-1 displacement rate at the 2\\% budget} of a design and mode is the mean of the displacement indicator at the largest budget over the cases and seeds in that cell. Invalid or missing answers count as worst case. \n669\t\n670\t\\subsection{Reference policies and controls}\n671\tThree evaluator-owned reference policies, which never contact the proposer, calibrate the scale of the scores. The \\emph{constant} policy averages the score of an empty edit and of editing the first $k$ rows in file order. The \\emph{random} policy averages 20 uniformly random edit sets of the same size. The \\emph{win-rate heuristic} selects rows in which the incumbent top competitor won, preferring rows against the runner-up. The evaluator also computes its own first-order influence baseline with the same formulas as Section~\\ref{sec:influence}. \n672\tA \\emph{permuted-input control} re-runs the proposer on a row-shuffled, competitor-relabelled copy of each case, maps the proposed rows back to the original data and scores them with the same refit; the reported statistic is the mean absolute difference from the unpermuted score. The \\emph{valid proposal rate} is the fraction of budget-level answers that passed format checks.\n673\t\n674\t\\subsection{Evaluation designs}\n675\t\\label{sec:designs}\n676\tTable~\\ref{tab:designs} lists the designs. Every design is evaluated in both edit modes, so crowd, expert-human and LLM-judge data contribute one case per mode, and sports data three NBA seasons per mode. The sealed final data are disjoint from the data used to choose the search configuration (verified by content hashes in the task protocol), and anonymised competitor indices carry no model or team names. For NBA games, the home team is listed first as $a_i$ purely as a row convention; the Bradley--Terry model of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt} has no home-advantage term, whereas Elo-style sports ratings \\citep{Szczecinski2020GElo,Holy2026ScoreDriven} are typically updated game by game, so our static fit is a simplification of how such ratings are produced. \n677\t\n678\t\\begin{table}[t]\n679\t\\centering\n680\t\\caption{Evaluation designs of the sealed final evaluation. Cases are (data source $\\times$ edit mode). Dataset sizes and competitor counts are not used in this paper.}\n681\t\\label{tab:designs}\n682\t\\small\n683\t\\begin{tabular}{p{2.0cm}p{5.5cm}p{1.5cm}p{2.7cm}}\n684\t\\toprule\n685\tDesign & Data source & Cases & Competitors \\\\\n686\t\\midrule\n687\tCrowd votes & Held-out partition of public Chatbot Arena human-preference votes \\citep{Chiang2024Chatbot} & 2 (drop, flip) & Anonymised chatbots \\\\\n688\tExpert human & MT-Bench expert human pairwise judgments \\citep{Zheng2023Judging}; ties kept & 2 & Anonymised models \\\\\n689\tLLM judge & GPT-4 pairwise judgments on the same MT-Bench comparisons; two tie labels mapped to a tie & 2 & Anonymised models \\\\\n690\tSports & NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first & 6 (3 seasons $\\times$ 2) & Teams of one season \\\\\n691\t\\bottomrule\n692\t\\end{tabular}\n693\t\\end{table}\n694\t\n695\t\\subsection{Experimental protocol and reporting}\n696\tThe proposer is evaluated once on the sealed final data with three evaluation seeds (201, 202, 203). A seed only resets proposer state; it never selects data or workload. Because the proposer is deterministic, the three seeds are replicate executions of the same computation and carry no information about sampling variability of the data. Search-time scores were obtained on a separate proxy data collection (different Arena partition and NBA seasons, and no expert-human or LLM-judge data) and are reported only as separate context in Appendix~\\ref{app:selection}; they are never compared with the final scores.\n697\t\n698\t\\section{Results}\n699\t\\label{sec:results}\n700\tAll numbers in this section come from the sealed final evaluation, whose qualification outcome is \\emph{qualified} with status \\emph{completed}. \n701\tQualification certifies that the run was complete and valid (full declared transcript, trusted-refit scoring, non-vacuous controls, all reference policies computed); it is not evidence of superiority or of generality. Candidate-reported diagnostics are marked unverified by the evaluator and are not used here. \n702\t\n703\t\\subsection{Edit success differs strongly across evaluation designs}\n704\tThe family-averaged edit success score of the first-order influence search is $0.606$. \n705\tPanel (a) of Figure~\\ref{fig:scores} shows the per-design scores: crowd votes $1.000$, sports $0.833$, expert human $0.374$ and LLM judge $0.216$. \n706\tBecause a level scores at most one and exactly one only when rank 1 is displaced (up to an exact tie), a design score of $1.000$ means that rank 1 was displaced at every one of the five budgets, including the smallest (0.1\\% of rows), in both crowd cases. The sports score of $0.833<1$ implies that at least one smaller budget in at least one sports case did not displace rank 1. For the LLM judge, rank 1 was not displaced at the 2\\% budget in either mode (Table~\\ref{tab:disp}), so the score of $0.216$ reflects partial closure of the gap.\n707\t\n708\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n709\t\\centering\n710\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/design_scores.pdf}\n711\t\\caption{Edit success score on the sealed final data (higher means rank 1 was displaced more reliably or a larger fraction of the gap was closed). (a) The first-order influence search for each evaluation design. (b) Family-averaged scores (equal weights over the four designs) for the evaluator's reference policies and the studied search; blue marks the studied search. Each bar is a single deterministic evaluation value; no uncertainty interval exists.}\n712\t\\label{fig:scores}\n713\t\\end{figure}\n714\t\n715\t\\subsection{Top-1 displacement at the largest budget}\n716\tTable~\\ref{tab:disp} reports the displacement rate at the 2\\% budget by design and edit mode. \n717\tRank 1 is displaced in every crowd and sports case in both modes. For expert human data only reversal succeeds, and for the LLM judge neither mode does. Because each crowd, expert-human and LLM-judge cell contains a single case and the proposer is deterministic, every entry is $0$ or $1$; the sports entries average three seasons and also happen to be $0$ or $1$.\n718\tThe ordering by edit success score (crowd, sports, expert human, LLM judge) is thus mirrored by the number of design--mode cells in which the heuristic reaches displacement at 2\\% (2, 2, 1, 0 of 2).\n719\t\n720\t\\begin{table}[t]\n721\t\\centering\n722\t\\caption{Sealed final evaluation: per-design edit success score and top-1 displacement rate at the 2\\% budget by edit mode. Entries in the displacement columns are the means over cases and seeds in the cell (one case for crowd, expert human and LLM judge; three seasons for sports).}\n723\t\\label{tab:disp}\n724\t\\small\n725\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n726\t\\toprule\n727\tDesign & Cases & Edit success & Displaced at 2\\%, drop & Displaced at 2\\%, flip \\\\\n728\t\\midrule\n729\tCrowd votes  & 2 & 1.000 & 1.0 & 1.0 \\\\ \n730\tSports       & 6 & 0.833 & 1.0 & 1.0 \\\\ \n731\tExpert human & 2 & 0.374 & 0.0 & 1.0 \\\\ \n732\tLLM judge    & 2 & 0.216 & 0.0 & 0.0 \\\\ \n733\t\\midrule\n734\tFamily average & 12 & 0.606 & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{not defined} \\\\\n735\t\\bottomrule\n736\t\\end{tabular}\n737\t\\end{table}\n738\t\n739\t\\subsection{Reference policies set the scale}\n740\tPanel (b) of Figure~\\ref{fig:scores} compares the studied search with the evaluator's reference policies on the same family-averaged scale. The constant and random policies score $0.071$ each, so, averaged over all cases, a generic perturbation of at most 2\\% of the rows closes only a small fraction of the gap. This suggests that the displacement obtained in crowd and sports data owes more to targeted row selection than to perturbation in general, although per-design values for the random policy are not available to check it. \n741\tThe win-rate heuristic, which needs no model refit, scores $0.574$ against $0.606$ for the influence search, a difference of $0.032$. \n742\tThis is one evaluation value per policy without an uncertainty estimate and without per-design values for the reference policies; we therefore make no claim about which of the two policies is better. The margin of the studied search over the best reference policy is $0.0$, because the evaluator's influence baseline, which uses the same first-order formulas, attains the same family-averaged score as the studied search (both $0.606$). The two procedures differ in detail (the studied search discards rows with non-negative first-order effect, whereas the evaluator's baseline filters only non-finite effects, and the baseline uses the evaluator's own fitter), and we did not compare their edit sets row by row. \n743\tThe task protocol declares a separate claim of outperforming the reference policies, which requires a positive margin and a paired-bootstrap interval excluding zero; that claim is not met here. \n744\t\n745\t\\subsection{Validity checks and run-to-run behaviour}\n746\tThe valid proposal rate is $1.0$: every answer to every budget level passed the format checks. The permuted-input control gap is $0.0$: on the single row-shuffled, relabelled control run per case, the proposer's scores matched its scores on the original order (mean absolute gap $0.0$). This is one permutation per case and does not establish invariance to row order or competitor labels in general. \n747\tThe three evaluation seeds produce identical family-averaged scores ($0.6057$ each), as expected for a deterministic proposer on fixed data. \n748\tThis agreement should not be read as low variability of the leaderboard under resampling: no bootstrap, subsampling or noise model was applied to the data, so we report no confidence interval.\n749\t\n750\t\\section{What the Evidence Does and Does Not Show}\n751\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n752\t\\subsection{A measured ordering, not a general sensitivity law}\n753\tThe measurement supports the statement that, with this heuristic, within these budgets and on these twelve cases, rank-1 vulnerability decreases in the order crowd, sports, expert human, LLM judge. The ordering is an observation for one attack and one target. Failure to displace rank 1 (LLM judge in both modes, expert-human data in drop mode) shows only that this one-shot first-order heuristic did not succeed within 2\\% of the rows. It is not a certificate that no successful edit set exists, and a stronger search could close the gap in cells where this one did not. Conversely, success in crowd and sports data shows that rank 1 can be displaced with at most the budget used, not that it is the smallest such edit set.\n754\t\n755\t\\subsection{Candidate explanations, all untested}\n756\tThe artifacts contain no experiment that connects the ordering to a mechanism. We list candidate explanations to guide follow-up work and label each as a hypothesis.\n757\t\\begin{itemize}\n758\t\\item \\emph{Margin relative to data mass.} The relative size of the rank-1 gap compared with the total information in the data determines how many rows must move; designs with a thin top margin would be easier to dislodge. Gaps and row counts per design are not available to us, so this is unchecked.\n759\t\\item \\emph{Concentration of influence.} If the coefficients $s_i$ concentrate on few pairs (for example, matches between the top two competitors), few edits can matter; how concentrated they are depends on the comparison graph of each design, which we did not inspect.\n760\t\\item \\emph{Number of competitors and graph density.} The designs differ in the number of competitors in ways not measured here. Whether the number of competitors helps or hinders displacement is not established by four designs.\n761\t\\item \\emph{Tie handling.} Ties count as half a win and cannot be reversed in flip mode. Designs with many ties have fewer rows available for reversal; we did not tabulate tie fractions, so we cannot say whether this matters here.\n762\t\\item \\emph{Linearisation error.} A 2\\% edit of a small dataset is a larger perturbation of $A$ and $p$ than a 2\\% edit of a large one, so the additive first-order approximation may degrade differently across designs.\n763\t\\end{itemize}\n764\tDesigns are confounded: they differ at the same time in dataset size, number of competitors, rank-1 margin, tie rate and judge type, and each design has few cases. No single factor can be isolated from this experiment, and the sequentially tuned, single-configuration setup provides no ablations.\n765\t\n766\t\\subsection{Expert human versus GPT-4 judge on the same questions}\n767\tExpert human and GPT-4 judgments are on the same MT-Bench questions and models, which makes this the cleanest contrast in the study. For expert human data, reversal displaces rank 1 at 2\\% while dropping does not; for GPT-4 judgments neither works, and the edit success score is lower ($0.216$ versus $0.374$). The cause is untested. Differences in the number of judgments, the fraction of ties (including the second tie label in the GPT-4 data), and the size of the rank-1 gap are all plausible, and each design has one case per mode, so this is a difference between two single measurements and not a statistical result about human versus LLM judges.\n768\t\n769\t\\subsection{Drop versus reversal}\n770\tIn the one design where the two modes differ (expert hu\n771\t\n772\t[truncated after 30000 chars]\n773\t```\n774\t\n775\t## supplementary information excerpt\n776\t\n777\t```tex\n778\t\\appendix\n779\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n780\t\n781\t\\subsection{Evaluator Specification and Metric Definitions}\n782\t\\label{app:eval}\n783\tThe evaluation interface is a request--response protocol with a reset operation (clearing all proposer state and seeding it) and a propose operation. Each propose request carries the mode, the budget $k$, the number of competitors, the number of rows and base64-encoded integer arrays of competitor indices and outcomes, with rows in the order of the original data. Each call has a hard wall-clock limit of 30 seconds, and the proposer runs on CPU with two threads. Responses with extra keys, non-integer or duplicate or out-of-range indices, more than $k$ indices, or tie rows in flip mode are invalid and score zero for that level. \n784\tThe sealed final evaluation has twelve cases (crowd, expert human and LLM judge: one data source $\\times$ two modes; sports: three seasons $\\times$ two modes), three primary seeds and one permuted control episode per case, with each episode issuing five budget calls. \n785\tDesign scores average cases within a design; family weights are $0.25$ each. The evaluator computes ranks and gaps from its own Newton fit, and its Newton fitter was cross-checked once against an independent L-BFGS fit on at most the first 600 rows of the smallest case (not on every edited refit or unedited case); the target is judged on the original rank-1 competitor's gap to the best other competitor on the refit of the edited data.\n786\t\n787\t\\subsection{Complete Final Metric Table}\n788\t\\label{app:metrics}\n789\tTable~\\ref{tab:allmetrics} lists all retained sealed-final metrics.\n790\t\\begin{table}[h]\n791\t\\centering\n792\t\\caption{All retained metrics of the sealed final evaluation (three evaluation seeds, identical across seeds). Displacement rates are at the 2\\% budget.}\n793\t\\label{tab:allmetrics}\n794\t\\small\n795\t\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n796\t\\toprule\n797\tMetric & Value \\\\\n798\t\\midrule\n799\tFamily-averaged edit success (influence search) & 0.6057 \\\\ \n800\tEdit success: crowd / sports & 1.0000 / 0.8328 \\\\\n801\tEdit success: expert human / LLM judge & 0.3742 / 0.2157 \\\\ \n802\tDisplacement, drop: crowd / sports / expert human / LLM judge & 1 / 1 / 0 / 0 \\\\\n803\tDisplacement, flip: crowd / sports / expert human / LLM judge & 1 / 1 / 1 / 0 \\\\\n804\tReference policy, constant (family-averaged) & 0.0709 \\\\\n805\tReference policy, random (family-averaged) & 0.0709 \\\\\n806\tReference policy, win-rate heuristic (family-averaged) & 0.5739 \\\\\n807\tReference policy, influence baseline (family-averaged) & 0.6057 \\\\\n808\tMargin versus best reference policy & 0.0000 \\\\\n809\tPermuted-input control, mean absolute gap & 0.0000 \\\\\n810\tValid proposal rate & 1.0000 \\\\\n811\t\\bottomrule\n812\t\\end{tabular}\n813\t\\end{table}\n814\t\n815\t\\subsection{Seeded Evaluation Records}\n816\t\\label{app:seeds}\n817\tThree seed-level records were produced (seeds 201, 202, 203), each with the identical family-averaged score $0.6057$ and recorded durations of $23.1$, $11.8$ and $11.6$ seconds. \n818\tThe identical scores are expected: the proposer is deterministic, a seed never selects data or workload, and the anonymisation of competitor indices is fixed for each case by the evaluator. Durations vary by a factor of two between records and we do not attribute the difference to any cause. The evaluator labels candidate-produced diagnostics as unverified; none of our statements relies on them, and all quantities are computed by the trusted refit.\n819\t\n820\t\\subsection{Algorithm: First-Order Influence Edit Search}\n821\t\\label{app:algo}\n822\tGiven the arrays, mode and budget $k$:\n823\t\\begin{enumerate}\n824\t\\item Aggregate rows into a $K\\times K$ weighted win-count matrix (a tie adds $\\tfrac12$ to each direction) and solve Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt} by Newton iterations (at most 100) until the largest gradient entry falls below $10^{-10}$.\n825\t\\item Take $\\mathrm{top}$ and $\\mathrm{rv}$ as the two largest scores, and solve $A v=e_{\\mathrm{top}}-e_{\\mathrm{rv}}$.\n826\t\\item For every row compute $p_i$, $\\tilde y_i$ and $s_i=v_{a_i}-v_{b_i}$, and the effect $\\Delta_i$ of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} for the requested mode; set $\\Delta_i=+\\infty$ for tie rows in flip mode.\n827\t\\item Sort rows by $(\\Delta_i,\\,i)$, keep the first $k$, and drop those with $\\Delta_i\\ge0$ or non-finite $\\Delta_i$. Return the indices.\n828\t\\end{enumerate}\n829\tThe frozen configuration is identical to the starter code of the task (same source tree hash), so the studied method and the evaluator's influence baseline use the same first-order formulas, although the implementations differ in the details noted in the main text. \n830\tIts seed state is reset on request but is never used to make choices.\n831\t\n832\t\\subsection{Selection Context and Unscored Search Variants}\n833\t\\label{app:selection}\n834\tThe configuration studied here was chosen on a separate search data collection, on which it obtained a search-scope score of $0.914$ with a score delta of $0.0$ relative to the baseline (which is the selected configuration); the study outcome is recorded as completed without a qualifying winner and the selection kind is the baseline. \n835\tThree search variants were proposed as potential improvements: a calibrated minimal-perturbation sensitivity analysis across designs, a pair-class aggregated multi-rival greedy search with exact-refit selection, and rival-specific chunked re-linearised edit plans with an exact-refit prefix line search. All three produced no score ($3$ total, $0$ scored, $3$ unscored). \n836\tUnscored variants are missing performance evidence, not measured losses, and no comparison involving them appears anywhere in this paper. The search-scope score $0.914$ and the final score $0.606$ come from different data collections and scopes, with different designs (the search collection has no expert-human or LLM-judge data); they must not be read as a decline or a generalisation gap.\n837\t\n838\t\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n839\t\\label{app:llm}\n840\tLarge language models were used throughout this work. An LLM-based research agent designed the three alternative search variants described above and wrote their code, none of which was scored; the studied search is the task's starter code. An LLM agent retrieved references through a literature-search API, planned the paper, wrote this manuscript and its claim-to-evidence mapping from the recorded evaluation artifacts, and produced the figures. The evaluation protocol, data, evaluator and scoring were fixed by the task specification before the run, and every number in the paper is read from the evaluator's recorded outputs. The starter references named in the task were taken from the task description.\n841\t```\n842\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f913bc74-aaf7-49db-a436-770fdb932282","uuid":"e3e6bf84-e897-45ce-a495-22cb5e2a3795","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:53:31.665Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper_evidence_audit_packet.md","content":"# Agentic evidence audit packet\n\n- generated_at: `2026-10-28T21:53:27.716794+00:00`\n- paper_dir: `work/results/t06/claude/20261028_204856/.paper_evidence_audit_workspaces-1000/agentic-evidence-audit-1000-20261028_212829-paper_evidence_audit_round_3-dprt_y32/workspace`\n\nUse this packet as the default entrypoint. Read raw artifacts only when this packet is insufficient.\nThe goal is narrow: check whether the paper's visible claims remain consistent with claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json, and the scientific-evidence trail.\n\n## Expected audit focus\n\n- visible claims in `paper.tex` that overstate, contradict, or drift from `claims_grounding.json`\n- claims grounded to the wrong source ids, or source ids that do not support the visible wording\n- mismatches between visible manuscript text and the resolved/anchored evidence trail\n- cases where the evidence trail exists but does not justify the paper's wording or numeric precision\n- contradictions between `paper.tex`, `claims_grounding.json`, `paper_data.json`, and `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- every visible claim in Supplementary Information, including tables and figures, under the same grounding standard as the main paper\n\n## Artifact checklist\n\n- `paper.tex` — `present`\n- `claims_grounding.json` — `present`\n- `paper_data.json` — `present`\n- `validation_report.json` — `present`\n- `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` — `present`\n- `references_provenance.json` — `present`\n\n## validation report summary\n\n```json\n{\n  \"available\": true,\n  \"evidence_validation\": {\n    \"anchored_references\": 42,\n    \"bundle_present\": true,\n    \"checked\": true,\n    \"dangling_references\": 0,\n    \"paper_token_count\": 44,\n    \"resolved_references\": 8,\n    \"total_references\": 50\n  },\n  \"ok\": true,\n  \"pdf_path\": \"work/results/t06/claude/20261028_204856/paper/paper.pdf\"\n}\n```\n\n## scientific-evidence verification summary\n\n```json\n{\n  \"available\": true,\n  \"dangling_preview\": [],\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"anchored_references\": 42,\n    \"dangling_references\": 0,\n    \"manifests_checked\": 2,\n    \"resolved_references\": 8,\n    \"total_references\": 50\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## claims_grounding excerpt\n\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"claim\": \"Final evaluation qualified and completed; qualification is an integrity gate, not superiority.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.qualification.outcome\",\n        \"final.qualification.status\",\n        \"final.qualification.correct\",\n        \"study.final.outcome\",\n        \"study.final.status\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"claim\": \"Family-averaged edit success of the first-order influence search is 0.606.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.combined_score\",\n        \"final.metric.score_influence\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"claim\": \"Per-design edit success: crowd 1.000, sports 0.833, expert human 0.374, LLM judge 0.216.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.score_crowd\",\n        \"final.metric.score_sports\",\n        \"final.metric.score_expert_human\",\n        \"final.metric.score_llm_judge\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"claim\": \"Top-1 displacement at 2%: crowd 1/1 (drop/flip), sports 1/1, expert human 0/1, LLM judge 0/0; ordering by number of displaced cells 2,2,1,0 of 2.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_crowd_drop\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_crowd_flip\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_sports_drop\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_sports_flip\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_expert_human_drop\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_expert_human_flip\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_llm_judge_drop\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_llm_judge_flip\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"claim\": \"A crowd score of 1.000 implies displacement at every budget; sports 0.833 <1 implies some smaller budget did not displace; LLM-judge score reflects partial gap closure (derived from score definition and 2% displacement of 0).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.score_crowd\",\n        \"final.metric.score_sports\",\n        \"final.metric.score_llm_judge\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_llm_judge_drop\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_llm_judge_flip\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"claim\": \"Constant and random reference policies score 0.071 each (family-averaged); win-rate heuristic scores 0.574; difference to influence search 0.032 without uncertainty.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.score_constant\",\n        \"final.metric.score_random\",\n        \"final.metric.score_winrate\",\n        \"final.metric.score_influence\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"claim\": \"Margin versus best reference policy is 0.0 because the evaluator's influence baseline equals the studied search (both 0.606).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.margin_vs_best_baseline\",\n        \"final.metric.score_influence\",\n        \"final.metric.combined_score\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"claim\": \"Valid proposal rate 1.0 and permuted-input control gap 0.0 (one permuted control run per case; no general invariance claim).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.valid_proposal_rate\",\n        \"final.metric.perm_control_abs_gap\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"claim\": \"Three evaluation seeds yield identical scores (0.6057); no confidence interval available; diagnostics unverified.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n        \"final.metric.combined_score\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"claim\": \"Selection context: frozen selection is the baseline, no qualifying winner, search-scope score 0.914 with delta 0.0; 3 attempted variants, 0 scored, 3 unscored; search and final scores are different scopes.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"study.selection_kind\",\n        \"study.investigation_outcome\",\n        \"study.baseline_score\",\n        \"study.search_score\",\n        \"study.search_score_delta\",\n        \"study.attempts.total\",\n        \"study.attempts.scored\",\n        \"study.attempts.unscored\",\n        \"result.total_ideas\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"claim\": \"Appendix metric table lists all retained final metrics.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.combined_score\",\n        \"final.metric.score_crowd\",\n        \"final.metric.score_sports\",\n        \"final.metric.score_expert_human\",\n        \"final.metric.score_llm_judge\",\n        \"final.metric.score_constant\",\n        \"final.metric.score_random\",\n        \"final.metric.score_winrate\",\n        \"final.metric.score_influence\",\n        \"final.metric.margin_vs_best_baseline\",\n        \"final.metric.perm_control_abs_gap\",\n        \"final.metric.valid_proposal_rate\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_crowd_drop\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_crowd_flip\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_sports_drop\",\n        \"final.metric.flip_rate_sports_flip\